'''''Tabernaemontana''''' is a genus of [[flowering plant]]s in the family [[Apocynaceae]]. It has a pan-tropical distribution, found in Asia, Africa, Australia, North America, South America, and islands of the Indian and Pacific Oceans.{{R|POWO|GBIF}} These plants are [[evergreen]] shrubs and small trees growing to 1–15 m tall. The leaves are arranged in opposite pairs, 3–25&nbsp;cm long, with milky sap; hence it is one of the diverse plant genera commonly called "'''milkwood'''". The flowers are fragrant, white, 1–5&nbsp;cm in diameter.

The cultivar ''[[Tabernaemontana divaricata|T. divaricata]]'' cv. 'Plena', with doubled-petaled flowers, is a popular houseplant.

Some members of the genus ''Tabernaemontana'' are used as additives to some versions of the psychedelic drink [[ayahuasca]];<ref>Ott (1995)</ref> the genus is known to contain [[ibogaine]] (e.g. in [[bëcchëte]], ''T. undulata''), [[conolidine]] (present in minor concentration in ''T. divaricata'')<ref>{{cite journal | doi=10.1002/cbdv.200490056 | title=Biologically Active Ibogan and Vallesamine Derivatives from ''Tabernaemontana divaricata'' | year=2004 | last1=Kam | first1=Toh-Seok | last2=Pang | first2=Huey-Shen | last3=Choo | first3=Yeun-Mun | last4=Komiyama | first4=Kanki | journal=Chemistry & Biodiversity | volume=1 | issue=4 | pages=646–656 | pmid=17191876 | s2cid=12805328 }}</ref> and  voacangine (''[[Tabernaemontana alba|T. alba]]'', ''[[Tabernaemontana arborea|T. arborea]]'', ''[[Tabernaemontana africana|T. africana]]'').<ref name="pmid27448833" /> Because of presence of [[coronaridine]] and [[voacangine]] in Mexican ''Tabernaemontana'' species,<ref name="pmid27448833">{{cite journal |vauthors=Krengel F, Herrera Santoyo J, Olivera Flores TJ, Chávez Ávila VM, Pérez Flores FJ, Reyes Chilpa R |title=Quantification of Anti-Addictive Alkaloids Ibogaine and Voacangine in ''In Vivo-'' and ''In Vitro-''Grown Plants of Two Mexican ''Tabernaemontana'' Species |journal=[[Chemistry & Biodiversity]] |volume=13 |issue=12 |pages=1730–1737 |date=December 2016 |pmid=27448833 |doi=10.1002/cbdv.201600146 |s2cid=46046257 |url= |issn=}}</ref> those plant could be used in economic production of anti-addictive alkaloids especially ibogaine and ibogamine.<ref name="pmid30618175">{{cite journal |vauthors=Krengel F, Chevalier Q, Dickinson J, Herrera Santoyo J, Reyes Chilpa R |title=Metabolite Profiling of Anti-Addictive Alkaloids from Four Mexican ''Tabernaemontana Species'' and the Entheogenic African Shrub ''Tabernanthe iboga'' (Apocynaceae) |journal=[[Chemistry & Biodiversity]] |volume=16 |issue=4 |article-number=e1800506 |date=April 2019 |pmid=30618175 |doi=10.1002/cbdv.201800506 |s2cid=58612529 |url= |issn=}} {{errata|doi=10.1002/cbdv.201900316|checked=yes}}</ref> ''[[Tabernaemontana sananho|T. sananho]]'' preparations are used in native medicine to treat eye injuries and as an [[anxiolytic]], and ''[[Tabernaemontana heterophylla|T. heterophylla]]'' is used to treat [[dementia]] in the elderly.<ref>Rodrigues & Carlini (2006)</ref>
Conolidine may be developed as a new class of pain killer.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.abc.net.au/science/articles/2011/05/23/3224191.htm |title = Scientists create new type of painkiller| website=[[Australian Broadcasting Corporation]] |date = 23 May 2011}}</ref>
[[Caterpillar]]s of the [[oleander hawk-moth]] (''Daphnis nerii'') have been found to feed on the [[pinwheelflower]] (''T. divaricata'').

==Taxonomy==
The genus was described by [[Carl Linnaeus]] and published in [[Species Plantarum]] 1: 210–211 in 1753. The [[type species]] is ''[[Tabernaemontana citrifolia|T. citrifolia]]''.

===Etymology===
The genus name commemorates the "father of German botany" [[Jacobus Theodorus Tabernaemontanus|Jakob Theodor von Bergzabern]], a.k.a. Jacobus Theodorus Tabernaemontanus, ''Tabernaemontanus'' being a compressed form of the original [[Medieval Latin]] name (''Tabernae Montanus'') of the botanist's home town of [[Bergzabern]] - both the Latin and the German forms of the town's name meaning "tavern(s) in the mountains".

